| Term            | Meaning                                  | Why it matters in this table                                                                                                          |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| GAN             | Generative Adversarial Network           | Trains a generator against a discriminator; strong for realistic sample synthesis, but can be unstable and suffer mode collapse.      |
| G vs D          | Generator vs Discriminator               | Core adversarial objective in GAN training; instability here drives common failure modes (collapse, oscillation).                     |
| cGAN            | Conditional GAN                          | Conditions generation on labels/attributes; useful for class balancing and targeted sample synthesis (e.g., specific attack classes). |
| ACGAN           | Auxiliary Classifier GAN                 | Adds an auxiliary classifier to improve controllability and class-conditional generation; may increase reliance on label quality.     |
| DCGAN           | Deep Convolutional GAN                   | Uses conv nets for generation/discrimination; good for image-like modalities (e.g., malware-as-images), less direct for sequences.    |
| WGAN            | Wasserstein GAN                          | Uses Wasserstein distance–motivated loss; often improves training stability and reduces mode collapse risk.                           |
| Adaptive GAN    | Online/evolving GAN variants             | Attempts to update as threats drift; raises validation and stability questions in real deployments.                                   |
| VAE             | Variational Autoencoder                  | Optimizes a variational objective; typically stable and good for representation learning, but may produce low-fidelity samples.       |
| ELBO            | Evidence Lower Bound                     | VAE training objective; trades off reconstruction quality vs latent regularization.                                                   |
| Transformer     | Attention-based architecture             | Strong for sequential/text data (logs, alerts); supports representation learning and generation depending on design.                  |
| LLM             | Large Language Model                     | Transformer scaled for language tasks; useful for summarization/analysis but introduces hallucination and prompt-injection risks.     |
| Auto-regressive | Next-token prediction training           | Enables text generation; produces fluent outputs but can hallucinate and follow malicious instructions without safeguards.            |
| Self-supervised | Training without manual labels           | Enables learning from unlabeled logs/alerts; often requires domain adaptation to be effective in cybersecurity.                       |
| Diffusion       | Denoising generative modeling            | Often high-quality generation and avoids mode collapse, but typically much more compute-heavy.                                        |
| Score matching  | Learning to denoise / estimate gradients | Common diffusion training paradigm; contributes to stability but increases training/inference cost.                                   |
